# caddy1 -- a first Caddyfile
#
# Caddy (written in Go) is a web server and reverse proxy with a very small
# config language. A site block starts with its address; `:PORT` means
# "any host, this port". Inside, directives such as `respond` handle
# requests, optionally limited by a matcher like a path:
#
#   respond /ping "pong" 200
#
# {$NAME} is replaced by the environment variable NAME when the file is
# loaded. ({NAME} without the $ is a *runtime* placeholder, and does not
# belong in a site address.)
#
# About the global options block at the top: Caddy normally runs an admin
# API on localhost:2019. `admin off` disables it, so several Caddy
# instances (yours, the checks') do not fight over that port.
#
# Task: listen on the port in $PORT;
#   GET /healthz  ->  200 "ok"
#   anything else ->  200 "hello from caddy"
#
#   PORT=8081 caddy run --config Caddyfile --adapter caddyfile
#   curl -i localhost:8081/healthz
# (in fish:  env PORT=8081 caddy run ...)
#

{
	admin off
}

:{$PORT} {
	respond /healthz "ok" 200
	respond "hello from caddy" 200
}
